this is my 23rd season breeding brassica  vegetables an effort that started mostly  with cabbage  and one that's morphed mostly into leafy  greens and  broccoli over the last 20 to 23 years  the  leafy green interest started around 2008  we were doing green bean work in  eastern africa with charles's phd  and seeing the importance of african  kale  and how that link to efforts we had  amongst the brassicas really started to  kick that off  the effort was helped with an academic  venture fund  support from the atkinson center  and that focused on trying to diversify  leafy greens in africa where we started  to look at  a lot of the  a lot of the market classes  that are available including the boracal  types cards and portuguese truncuda and  toscano types  so that involved  testing materials in kenya and tanzania  and getting a feel for some of the  diversity that exists amongst the  brassica leafy greens which is  considerable  that um  led initially into testing a lot of the  collard collection from the usda around  2009  2010  i'm trying to identify some  unique quality traits within that  collection  uh that led to  selecting some golden colors out of the  cabbage colored types in that collection  and over time moving the golden leaf  color into the curly  flatten survive types that are more  indicative of the kel markets  we also initiated around 2012 efforts on  natural colors from brassica  uh this was really identified in high  amphis ion and materials  ones that  form desirable colors into the ph six to  eight  range  and brassicas are very high in  cyanidants  so we basically initiated efforts  in  the golden leaf  leafy greens  and in the high anthocyanin  purple materials  the popularity of kale over that time  has  increased  dramatically  this started around 10 years ago  and the reasons for it are multiple it's  a very versatile vegetable  uh it's got more texture and integrity  so it's a better component for  chips and smoothies  also makes an alternative base for  salads that can typically be more  complex  it's also noted for having high levels  of vitamins and minerals  the types that have  become more popularized in the us are  the  uh the curly types and the toscano types  they both have high levels of  leaf texture  and that gives a  number of benefits in the market it  provides a crunch rather than a crisp  than you get from lettuce  it provides a lot of volume in the salad  by giving it the shape characteristics  and of course it gives it the ability to  hold dressing very well rather than  slipping off like it would on a flat  leaf collard leaf type  so cur kayla's a little bit of a generic  term because it can cross species but  essentially  kale is in four primary groups i would  say it's in the type curl boracal type  it's in the uh savoy crinkle leaf uh  toscano or lacinato type  you have the flat leaf types which  include the forage kales thousand-headed  kale  and the collards and then  less common you have the  the jagged leaf types you can get these  in the olyraciotypes and i've been  working on them certainly  uh like fez uh but most of these are the  russian and siberian types which are  actually uh brass canapus not brassica  or  so the cat the salad types which are  more fit in with modern markets really  form in with the curl and the savoy that  you get from  the curly leaf and the toscano leaf  types  the market classes  also um seem to vary  it's not that they have better or worse  nutrition they have different nutrition  this is a preliminary study um hannah  sweygarden who was a recent phd student  and  carl sams from the  analysis for these down in tennessee  uh but they did give indications that  collards tend to be  higher in the  glucosinolate  categories  compared to the  curly and toscano types  and the  toscano and curly types tend to be  higher in the carotenoids than the  collar types  so  with among these market classes you have  different colors different leaf  morphologies  different nutrition levels and you can  create a type of a fusion breeder where  you can move some of these  characteristics among the traditional  market classes to  provide some new market opportunities  the  effort on leaf color has fallen  basically into the aesthetic realm and  the nutrition realm and is focused on  the  the more green golden green types and  the anthocyanin types  and  this gives nutrition  via the  polyphenolics and the anthocyanins and  via the carotenoids and the  glucosinolates just in the regular  leaf types of brassicas of course  part of it is  diversifying from a fairly limited  market class  to scholar  it's characterized as having this dark  blue green type as many kales a lot of  people see this as high levels of  nutrition  where a lot of the standard market  classes are more this golden green type  um  remain arugula etc so part of it's  keeping the crunch and dressing holding  characteristics of the toscano but  moving it into perhaps more mainstream  market classes and even focusing on  these deep purple or purple vein kale  types in those categories  the toscano cal has a number of benefits  it's it's more self-compatible than the  curly kale so it's a little bit easier  to work with in a breeding program  especially if you're looking to develop  things like cms hybrids so  these toscano kales have been crossed  out to multiple brassica species over  the last 10 years and  a diverse set has basically been  developed of inbred lines  during that time we've also developed  a cms line that shows good  complementation in hybrid production  as a as a standard to  do complementation tests of  tuscan pollinators  uh we've also been  selecting the savoy type and a sort of  toscana collard type of background  amongst breeding lines which you can see  has much more vigor than this  toscano leaf in this image  so in 2020  uh 27  breeding lines in red lines  uh were identified that ranged in leaf  shape with texture leaf color from  golden to purple  to bright green  and they were all used as pollinators  onto a common  cms  seed parent at the same time this cmsc  period was increased with the maintainer  with the seed company to develop a  million or so seeds in  preparation for  seed production  so these 27 hybrids were produced and  now we're identifying  based on vigor  appearance and uniformity  a range of leaf types the color  typically is intermediate  to the two parents the  cms um  see parent and the pollinators are on  the right  so this is an example of a  a selection of the blue green leaf type  with the tkp2  then if we were to use the bright green  tkp 16  with the blue green we end up with more  of a dark green look  if we use a  golden pollinator  we end up with a a bright green or a  golden green color  and if we use a purple pollinator we end  up with a  purple leaf tuscan and of course  these all share they're all half hazards  because they all share the parents and  so  they will have some commonality with  their  production and maturity and appearance  just based on that criteria  so  that's the work that's currently in  progress and we're also of course  starting to look at toscano college and  this is where we backtrack to the  african efforts perhaps  um a lot of sakura wiki has more of this  flat leaf herbaceous type so the shelf  life is very limited to just a few hours  before it has to be used of course  when you get this avoid type into both  parents that are being combined  uh in this case an inbred of uh survivor  crossed to a  bright green  tuscan  you can see how they can complement one  another to to really get bigger that's  five times what the inbred parent says  and provide a very nice  looking product that's going to cook  down better to a larger amount  and going to have a lot more shelf  stability in the marketplace and there's  already a  hybrid in eastern africa we've found me  from east african seats that has a  little bit of survive so  that's  already been shown to be popular in the  market the savoy is more like the  leaf type up here  so the effort now is to really  try and move a lot of these  purple and golden leaf traits across the  brassica market classes  and toscano has been a  start of course  um it's a  it's something that needs various steps  you need to select the best golden types  you need to get them into the right  plant types then you need to do the cms  conversion  so once you get that into two parents to  complement one another you can actually  get some very nice  materials  uh we're doing a cms conversion of tkp18  right now to be able to develop this and  anecdotally what we find from these  types of products is we hear that  they're um  more tender not as bitter  attractive  and so they have different  market class characteristics that can be  appealing to  different groups perhaps even the  southeast  colored  consumers  the  toscano is the going to be the first  group that's coming out of course the  traditional toscano is the blue green  uh but moving the purple vein into a um  dark  green or blue-green leaf  currently moving it also into the golden  green leaf  it ultimately provides the  um  a diverse set  of toscano types and next-gen set so to  speak  which  can be half sims or very close uh so can  have similar horticultural  characteristics and then can be chopped  up to produce sort of rainbow lacinata  sound mixes for example or used of  course in the baby green sets as  masonito tends to be a  tuscan tuscan tends to be a very good  seed producer  so this has come through the combination  of multiple efforts and  funding sources but most importantly the  ability to  work with some flexibility as a breeder  without being  tied into  very rigid criteria  and so that's just an overview of my  next-gen toscana work and if anybody has  any questions i'll be happy to answer  them via the zoom or via email  thank you very much
 

